    "notes": "Edition: OCW Fall 2022 (the only OCW edition of 6.100L found). 6.100L is the slower full-semester version of 6.100A (formerly 6.0001); textbook Guttag, Introduction to Computation and Programming Using Python, 3rd ed. A Fall 2025 introcomp.mit.edu site exists (described as 'the old 6.100L') but is behind MIT login, so not used; the MIT catalog has since reorganized intro CS (6.100A/6.100B/6.1000). Lecture titles are verbatim from 'Materials by Lecture'; module topics are taken from the OCW Calendar topic column (lecture slides were not individually parsed), so per-lecture concept lists are inferred from those titles/topics and are approximate. Problem sets are attached to the lecture at which they were released per the calendar (PS0 L1, PS1 L2, PS2 L9, PS3 L12, PS4 L16, PS5 L20); PS concepts come from the PS PDFs (PS1 compound interest incl. bisection search; PS2 Hangman; PS3 Document Distance with dictionaries/TF-IDF; PS4 recursion on trees + Caesar/one-time-pad cipher with Message class hierarchy; PS5 Using Libraries: PIL colorblindness filters and image steganography). Lecture 25 'Plotting' assumed to use matplotlib (calendar says 'visualization library'). Concepts 'random_module' and 'simulation_monte_carlo' in L26 inferred from 'Simulations'. PS concepts taught in later lectures (e.g. bisection in PS1 Part C, classes/inheritance in PS4 Parts B\u2013C) are attached to the lecture that teaches them rather than the release lecture. Concept first_module reflects first appearance including problem-set concepts (e.g. file_io via Hangman word loading at L9)."
